# Definitions and Main Theorem @=sec:definitions
For an abelian group $G$, a ***prototile*** is a finite nonempty subset
$F\subset G$.  Its translated copies are the sets $a+F$.
Following the tiling-equation notation of
\cite[Section~1.1]{GTUndecidable}, for subsets $A,F$ of an abelian
group $G$, the notation $A\oplus F=G$
means that every element of $G$ has exactly one representation as $a+f$
with $a\in A$ and $f\in F$.  We say that $F$ ***tiles*** $G$ if such an $A$
exists, and call $A$ its ***set of translations***.
The prototile is finite, but its set of translations is infinite when $G$
is infinite.  We allow translations only.  Intermediate prototiles may be
disconnected; the final prototile will be connected.
A finite set $F\subset\Z^d$ is ***connected*** if every pair of its
points can be joined by a sequence of points of $F$, successive points
differing by a signed coordinate unit vector.
Connectedness of polycubes and polyominoes always refers to this lattice
adjacency, including when they are viewed as unions of unit cubes.
A ***polycube*** is a finite nonempty subset of $\Z^3$, or its corresponding
union of unit cubes; a ***polyomino*** is its two-dimensional counterpart,
following the terminology of \cite[Section~1.2]{Kim}.
A tiling using a single prototile is a ***translational monotiling***.
More generally, $B\oplus C=E$ denotes unique representation of every
point of $E$ as a sum, with no sums outside $E$; $B+C$ denotes the set
of sums without a uniqueness condition, and $B-C$ is the set of
differences $b-c$.  For a vector $u$ and an integer $n$, write
$u+B=\{u+b:b\in B\}$ and $nB=\{nb:b\in B\}$.
The notation $\sqcup$ denotes a disjoint union.
A ***simultaneous tiling system*** consists of a finite family of prototiles
$F_1,\ldots,F_s$ in a common target space $G$ and asks for one set of
translations $A$ satisfying $A\oplus F_\nu=G$ for every $\nu$,
as in \cite[Section~3]{GTPeriodic}.
Each prototile must individually tile the entire target space using the
same set of translations.

For a finite nonempty $F\subset\Z^d$, its ***unit-cube union*** is
$F+[0,1]^d$.  In $\R^d$, a ***tiling by translations*** means that each point
belongs to exactly one translated copy, except on a set of Lebesgue
measure zero; this is coverage with multiplicity one almost everywhere
\cite[Section~1.1]{GTPeriodic}.

A subgroup $\Lambda$ has ***finite index*** when there are finitely many
***cosets*** $g+\Lambda$; the group of these cosets is the
***quotient*** $G/\Lambda$.
A set $A$ is ***invariant*** under $\Lambda$ if $A+\lambda=A$ for every
$\lambda\in\Lambda$.  We call a set of translations ***periodic*** if it is
invariant under a finite-index subgroup, following
\cite[Section~1.1]{GTPeriodic}.

A ***Wang tile*** is a unit square with a color on each of its east, north,
west, and south edges; a ***Wang tiling*** assigns tiles from a finite set
to $\Z^2$, without rotating or reflecting them, with equal colors on
touching edges.  A Wang tile set tiles $\Z^2$ when such an assignment
exists \cite[Definition~2.6]{GTUndecidable}.

A decision problem is ***decidable*** if an algorithm halts on every input
with the correct yes or no answer.  It is ***recursively enumerable*** (RE)
if an algorithm halts exactly on its yes instances, and
***co-recursively enumerable*** (co-RE) if an algorithm halts exactly on its
no instances.
A ***computable many-one reduction*** is a computable map from inputs of one
problem to inputs of another, preserving yes and no
answers.  A problem is ***co-RE-hard*** if every co-RE problem has such a
reduction to it, and ***co-RE-complete*** if it is also in co-RE.
For an encoding, ***completeness*** means that an original solution yields
an encoded solution; ***soundness*** means that an encoded solution implies
an original solution.

: theorem @=thm:main
  There is a computable map from finite nonempty Wang tile sets $\calT$
  to finite nonempty connected sets $F_{\calT}\subset\Z^3$ such that
  $$
   \calT\text{ tiles }\Z^2
   \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
   F_{\calT}\text{ tiles }\Z^3\text{ by translations}.
  $$
  Consequently, translational monotiling of $\Z^3$ is co-RE-complete
  under computable many-one reductions, even when the input prototile is
  required to be connected.

# The Two-Prime Sudoku Rule @=sec:sudoku
We use $\N=\{0,1,2,\ldots\}$ and the domino-system formulation of
\cite[Definition~2.2]{GTUndecidable}.  A ***domino system*** consists of a finite
nonempty alphabet $\calT$ and two allowed-pair relations
$H,V\subseteq\calT^2$.  A ***solution*** on a subset of $\Z^2$ assigns a
symbol $\tau(u,v)$ to each point, with
$$
 (\tau(u,v),\tau(u+1,v))\in H,\qquad
 (\tau(u,v),\tau(u,v+1))\in V
$$
whenever both points of the pair are in the domain.
For a finite Wang tile set, take its tiles as $\calT$, put $(t,t')\in H$
when the east color of $t$ equals the west color of $t'$, and put
$(t,t')\in V$ when the north color of $t$ equals the south color of $t'$.
This correspondence is recorded in
\cite[Definition~2.6 and equations~(2.1)--(2.2)]{GTUndecidable}.

Solvability on $\N^2$ is equivalent to solvability on $\Z^2$
\cite[Lemma~2.5]{GTUndecidable}.
Indeed, a solution on the quadrant provides solutions on every finite
square by restriction and translation; compactness then gives a
solution on the plane.  Restriction proves the reverse implication.
We use the classical co-RE-completeness of this domino problem [[Berger,GTUndecidable]].

For a prime $r$, write $\F_r=\Z/r\Z$ for its field of residues and
$\F_r^\times$ for the nonzero residues.
Fix distinct primes $p,q>200$ once and for all, and set
$$
 N=p^2q^2,\qquad I=\{0,\ldots,N-1\},\qquad
 \Sigma=\F_p^\times\times\F_q^\times\times\calT.
$$
The width $N$ is fixed independently of the Wang tile set.  The alphabet
has $(p-1)(q-1)|\calT|$ symbols and therefore depends on that set.
The finite-width board with constraints on all integer-slope lines is
the Sudoku framework of Greenfeld and Tao
\cite[Definition~7.4]{GTPeriodic}; we use its general word-rule
formulation \cite[Definition~3.1]{GTUndecidable}.
A ***word*** on $I$ is a function $I\to\Sigma$; $\Sigma^I$ denotes the set
of all such words.  A ***word rule*** is a subset $\calW\subseteq\Sigma^I$.
An ***array*** is a function $W:I\times\Z\to\Sigma$.
It is a ***Sudoku array*** for the word rule $\calW$ if
: equation \label{eq:line-rule}
   (W(n,dn+e))_{n\in I}\in\calW
   \qquad(d,e\in\Z).
Write $W=(W_p,W_q,W_{\calT})$ for its three components.
The first two are its ***integer components***, taking values in
$\{1,\ldots,p-1\}$ and $\{1,\ldots,q-1\}$, respectively, identified with
the corresponding nonzero residues.  The third is its ***Wang tile label***.
The ***column*** at $n$ is the function $m\mapsto W(n,m)$ on $\Z$;
***nonconstancy*** of an integer component means that this component takes
at least two different values in the column; repetitions are allowed.
We additionally require both integer components to be nonconstant on
every column.  This requirement is separate from the Sudoku word rule.

For a nonzero integer $m$, let $\nu_p(m)$ denote the largest integer
$k\geq0$ for which $p^k$ divides $m$, called its ***$p$-adic valuation***,
and let $f_p(m)$ be the residue of $m/p^{\nu_p(m)}$ in
$\F_p^\times$.  Set $\nu_p(0)=+\infty$ and $f_p(0)=1$, and use the
analogous notation for $q$.
The ***last-nonzero-digit function*** and the convention at zero follow
\cite[Definition~4.1]{GTUndecidable}.

## The Finite Word Rule

We now define $\calW$ explicitly, following
\cite[Definition~5.1]{GTUndecidable}.
For integers $a,b$, call $\lambda(n)=an+b$ ***nondegenerate modulo $r$***
if $a,b$ are not both divisible by $r$, and put
$$
 t_r(a)=\begin{cases}1,&r\nmid a,\\0,&r\mid a.\end{cases}
$$
A word $w=(w_p,w_q,w_{\calT})\in\Sigma^I$ is ***allowed*** if there are
integers $a,b$ such that $\lambda$ is nondegenerate modulo both primes,
and a domino solution
$$
 \sigma\colon \{0,\ldots,t_p(a)\}\times\{0,\ldots,t_q(a)\}\to\calT,
$$
such that, for every $n\in I$,
: equation \label{eq:explicit-word-rule}
  \begin{aligned}
   w_r(n)&=f_r(\lambda(n))
      &&\text{if }\nu_r(\lambda(n))\leq t_r(a),\quad r=p,q,\\
   w_{\calT}(n)&=\sigma(\nu_p(\lambda(n)),\nu_q(\lambda(n)))
      &&\text{if both valuation bounds hold.}
  \end{aligned}
The coordinates not covered by these conditions are unrestricted.
The domain of $\sigma$ has one, two, or four points, and its domino
constraints are exactly the $H,V$ tests for adjacent points defined above.

This is a finite, effective definition.  All valuation tests and required
residues in \eqref{eq:explicit-word-rule} depend only on $a,b$ modulo
$p^2q^2$: a valuation at most one and the corresponding last nonzero
digit are determined modulo $r^2$.  Enumerate these coefficient residues,
all assignments $\sigma$ on the corresponding rectangle, and all words
obeying their constraints.  Taking their union computes $\calW$ without
an infinite search or a domino solution.

The valuations provide the two coordinates of the simulated domino
plane.  Given a domino solution $\tau$ on $\N^2$, decorate each height
$m\ne0$ with the tile $\tau(\nu_p(m),\nu_q(m))$.  After removing common prime
powers from the coefficients of a line, its low-valuation points test
a rectangle of adjacent domino positions.  The integer components
record last nonzero digits so that the tests remain consistent across
different lines and valuation scales.  The reverse assertion, that
these local tests and nonconstant columns force a global domino
solution, is the substantive theorem we import.

## The Imported Soundness Theorem and a Canonical Solution

: theorem[Decorated Sudoku, Greenfeld--Tao] @=thm:GT
  The word rule just defined has the following properties.
  : enumerate[label=\textup{(\roman*)}]
    \item If an array satisfies \eqref{eq:line-rule} and both $W_p$ and $W_q$
    are nonconstant on every column, then the domino system has a solution
    on $\N^2$.
    \item If $\tau\colon\N^2\to\calT$ is a domino solution, choose any
    $t_*\in\calT$ and define the array
    : equation \label{eq:canonical-sudoku}
       W(n,m)=
       \begin{cases}
       \bigl(f_p(m),f_q(m),\tau(\nu_p(m),\nu_q(m))\bigr),&m\ne0,\\
       (1,1,t_*),&m=0,
       \end{cases}
    Then $W$ satisfies \eqref{eq:line-rule}, and both integer components
    are nonconstant on every column.

The array in \eqref{eq:canonical-sudoku} is called the
***canonical Sudoku array*** associated to $\tau$.
Part \textup{(i)} is the implication of
\cite[Proposition~5.2]{GTUndecidable} that we use as a black box.
For completeness, we verify part \textup{(ii)} directly.
On the line $m=dn+e$ with $(d,e)\ne(0,0)$, put
$$
 h_r=\min\{\nu_r(d),\nu_r(e)\},\qquad
 d_r=d/r^{h_r},\qquad e_r=e/r^{h_r}\quad(r=p,q).
$$
The Chinese remainder theorem allows residues to be prescribed
independently modulo coprime integers.  Choose $a,b$ so that
$a\equiv d_r$, $b\equiv e_r\pmod{r^2}$ for both primes.
The form $an+b$ is nondegenerate modulo each prime.  Whenever its
$r$-valuation is at most $t_r(a)$, that valuation and its last nonzero
digit agree with those of $d_rn+e_r$.  Multiplication by $r^{h_r}$
does not change the last nonzero digit, and adds $h_r$ to the valuation.
Thus
$$
 \sigma(u,v)=\tau(h_p+u,h_q+v)
 \quad(0\leq u\leq t_p(a),\ 0\leq v\leq t_q(a))
$$
witnesses every constraint in \eqref{eq:explicit-word-rule}.
At a point where $dn+e=0$, both valuation bounds fail, so its arbitrary
decoration causes no problem.  For the line $d=e=0$, choose $a=0,b=1$
and $\sigma(0,0)=t_*$.  This proves the line rule for the entire array.
Both integer components are nonconstant on every column: heights
$1$ and $2$ already give the distinct values $1$ and $2$.

### A Small Arithmetic Example

Let $A$ have east and north colors $0$ and west and south colors $1$,
and let $B$ have the opposite colors. These two Wang tiles force a
checkerboard, with $\tau(u,v)=A$ when $u+v$ is even and $B$ otherwise.
@fig:sudoku-example shows how the valuation pair addresses this
tiling in the canonical array. For readability, the figure uses the
toy primes $p=3,q=5$, as in the arithmetic illustration of
\cite[Figure~5.1]{GTUndecidable}. The canonical formula and its
line-rule verification above apply to these primes, but we make no
soundness claim for them: @thm:GT uses $p,q>200$.
Every displayed height has the same symbol in every column $n$.
For example, height $45$ gives the full symbol $(2,4,B)$, because
$f_3(45)=2$, $f_5(45)=4$, and $(\nu_3(45),\nu_5(45))=(2,1)$.
This illustrates the arithmetic interface, rather than enumerating
the much larger lattice prototile produced by the full reduction.

