### The Two Seed Symbol Sets

Parametrize the lines by $x=(x_1,x_2)\in\Z^2$, and put
$$
 L_n(x)=x_2+nx_1,\qquad u_n=(1,-n).
$$
Then $L_n$ is onto $\Z$ and its ***kernel***, the set of points mapped to
zero, is $\ker L_n=\Z u_n$.
The two auxiliary constraints will use the symbol sets
: equation \label{eq:seed-symbols}
   B_t=\{(t,t,a):a\in\calT\}\subset\Sigma,
   \qquad t=1,2.
Here the two occurrences of $t$ are interpreted in the respective fields.
In particular, $B_1$ and $B_2$ are disjoint.

The canonical array has a useful uniform property:
: equation \label{eq:canonical-seed}
   x_1\equiv0,\ x_2\equiv t\pmod{pq}
   \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
   W(n,L_n(x))\in B_t\quad(n\in I).
Indeed, $L_n(x)$ has the nonzero residue $t$ modulo both primes.
No constraint on the third coordinate is needed in this implication.

: remark
  The nonconstancy hypothesis concerns each integer component, not
  merely the triple $W$.  Variation of the Wang label alone would not
  suffice.  This is the reason for imposing \eqref{eq:seed-symbols}.

# Encoding Finite Constraints with a Cyclic Factor @=sec:encoding
The general method of encoding finite-valued functional constraints
by tiling equations was developed by Greenfeld and Tao
\cite[Sections~4--8]{GTTwoTiles} and
\cite[Theorem~4.1]{GTPeriodic}.
The graph and cycle gadgets in this section adapt
\cite[Section~3]{OpenAI2026}.  We give their definitions and proofs for
the decorated alphabet, and keep the prime choices explicit.
OpenAI used this encoding for the one-prime Sudoku rule of
\cite[Section~4]{GTUndecidable}.  Here we adapt it to the two-prime
Sudoku array problem, including the Wang tile constraints and both
nonconstant-column conditions.

## Coordinates and the Graph Equation
A ***channel*** is an indexed collection of input and output coordinates.
Let $\mathcal I=I\sqcup\{\eta_1,\eta_2\}$ be their index set.
Indices $n\in I$ are ***ordinary channels***; $\eta_1,\eta_2$ are
***seed channels***, used to enforce the two auxiliary constraints.
The ***label set*** of channel $i$ is denoted by $J_i$: put $J_n=\Sigma$
for ordinary channels and $J_{\eta_t}=\{*\}$ for seeds.
Set
: equation \label{eq:residues}
   M=(pq)^2,\qquad S=(\Cyc M)^2,\qquad D=M^2=(pq)^4,
   \qquad s(x)=x\bmod M.
For each channel $i$ choose primes $a_i,b_i$, all mutually distinct,
different from $p,q$, with
$$
 a_{\eta_1}=2,\qquad a_{\eta_2}=3,\qquad
 b_{\eta_1},b_{\eta_2}>D.
$$
Write $P_i=(\Cyc{a_i})\times(\Cyc{b_i})$.
For every $j\in J_i$ choose nonnegative integers $A_{ij},B_{ij}$
and a further prime $r_{ij}$, distinct from all other chosen primes,
such that
: equation \label{eq:semigroup}
   r_{ij}=A_{ij}a_i+B_{ij}b_i,\qquad A_{ij},B_{ij}\geq0.
This is effective: if $r\geq(a_i-1)b_i$, choose
$B\in\{0,\ldots,a_i-1\}$ with $Bb_i\equiv r\pmod{a_i}$,
and put $A=(r-Bb_i)/a_i$.  Searching for sufficiently large fresh
primes therefore terminates.

All products indexed by channels range over $\mathcal I$.  Let
$$
 r_i=\prod_{j\in J_i}r_{ij},\qquad
 K_i=\Cyc{r_i}=\prod_{j\in J_i}\Cyc{r_{ij}},\qquad
 K=\prod_i K_i,\qquad P=\prod_iP_i,
$$
where the product description of $K_i$ uses the Chinese remainder
isomorphism.  Each coordinate in this product is called an ***input digit***.
Denote by $U_{ij}$ the subgroup with arbitrary $j$th
digit and all other digits zero; subtraction by an element of $U_{ij}$
changes only that digit.
The ***target space*** is the abelian group
: equation \label{eq:ambient}
   G=\Z^2\times V,\qquad
   V=(\Cyc D)\times\prod_i\bigl((\Cyc{r_i^2})\times P_i\bigr).
The cyclic orders $D,r_i^2,a_i,b_i$ appearing here are pairwise coprime,
so $V$ is cyclic.  The residue set $S$ is only an indexing set for
functions; it is not a factor of $V$.
Identifying $V$ with $\Cyc m$, where $m=|V|$, gives the target space
$\Z^2\times(\Z/m\Z)$: a 3D integer grid with a finite coordinate that
wraps around modulo $m$.  This coordinate encodes all the finite factors
displayed above; the auxiliary output $z$ below takes values in just the
factor $\Cyc D$.

Write coordinates of $G$ as $(x,z,(v_i,c_i)_i)$, and project to the
***base*** $(x,k)\in\Z^2\times K$ by setting $k_i=v_i\bmod r_i$.
We call $x\in\Z^2$ the ***horizontal coordinates***.
The $k_i$ are the ***low coordinates***; $c_i\in P_i$ are the ***useful outputs***,
and $z\in\Cyc D$ is the ***auxiliary output***.  A ***fiber*** over a base point
is the set of all points projecting to it; a ***lift*** of a base point is
any point in that fiber.
The kernel of this projection is the finite subgroup
$$
 H_0=\{0\}\times(\Cyc D)\times
 \prod_i\bigl(r_i(\Cyc{r_i^2})\times P_i\bigr).
$$
Our first equation is
: equation \label{eq:graph}
   A\oplus H_0=G.
It says exactly that $A$ has one point over each base point $(x,k)$.
We call such an $A$ a ***graph over the base***; it is specified by its
output functions $z,c_i,v_i$.
For a residue $t$ modulo a positive integer $r$, write $[t]_r$ for its
least nonnegative integer representative.
It is useful to replace $v_i$ by an arbitrary function
$\beta_i:\Z^2\times K\to K_i$, called its ***high output***, via
: equation \label{eq:shear}
   v_i(x,k)=x_1+[k_i-x_1]_{r_i}+r_i\beta_i(x,k)
   \quad\text{in }\Cyc{r_i^2}.
This parametrizes the possible lifts of $k_i$ bijectively.  It is a choice
of coordinates on fibers, not a splitting of cyclic groups.

## Controlling Dependence
We next require functions
$\widetilde c_n:\Z\times K_n\to P_n$ and
$\widetilde c_{\eta_t}:S\times K_{\eta_t}\to P_{\eta_t}$ such that
: equation \label{eq:dependence}
   c_n(x,k)=\widetilde c_n(L_n(x),k_n),\qquad
   c_{\eta_t}(x,k)=\widetilde c_{\eta_t}(s(x),k_{\eta_t}).
These will not restrict $z$ or any $\beta_i$.
The dependence gadget is \cite[Lemma~3.1]{OpenAI2026}, with our
decorated channel coordinates.

: lemma @=lem:invariance
  For a channel $i$ and a base shift $\sigma\in\Z^2\times K$, a finite
  prototile can impose $c_i(b-\sigma)=c_i(b)$ for every base point $b=(x,k)$ on graphs
  satisfying \eqref{eq:graph}, without restricting their other outputs.
: proof
  Let $\pr_i:G\to P_i$ be the coordinate projection.
  Partition $H_0$ into $E_0=\{h:\pr_i(h)=0\}$ and $E_*=H_0\setminus E_0$.
  Choose a lift $g$ of $\sigma$ with $\pr_i(g)=0$, and use
  $F=E_*\sqcup(g+E_0)$.  The union is disjoint by its $c_i$ coordinates.
  At base $b$, the first part covers all slices of the fiber except the one
  whose $i$th useful output is $c_i(b)$.  The second part covers exactly
  the slice with $i$th useful output $c_i(b-\sigma)$.  Every other
  coordinate is filled freely and once in either contribution.
  Exact coverage is therefore equivalent to the stated identity.

Apply this lemma to generators of $K_\ell$ for every $\ell\ne i$,
then to the horizontal shift $u_n$ in ordinary channel $n$, and to
the two shifts $(M,0),(0,M)$ in each seed channel.  These finitely
many equations are equivalent to \eqref{eq:dependence}.

For a graph with these dependencies, put $g_{i,x}(k_i)=c_i(x,k)$.
Following \cite[Section~3.3]{OpenAI2026}, define its ***active labels*** by
: equation \label{eq:active}
   \calA_i(x)=\{j\in J_i:\exists v,v'\in K_i,
         \ v-v'\in U_{ij},\ g_{i,x}(v)\ne g_{i,x}(v')\}.
A label is ***inactive*** when the function ignores that input digit.
A channel is ***active*** at $x$ if $\calA_i(x)\ne\varnothing$, and
***inactive*** at $x$ otherwise.
If all labels are inactive, the function is constant: change the
digits one at a time between any two inputs.
For ordinary $n$, the active set depends only on $L_n(x)$, and for
a seed it depends only on $s(x)$.

## Excluding a Tuple of Active Labels
The next test is the cycle construction of
\cite[Lemma~3.2]{OpenAI2026}.
: lemma @=lem:cycle
  Let $C$ be a nonempty cyclically ordered list of distinct channels,
  and choose one label $j(i)\in J_i$ for each $i\in C$.  A finite family of finite
  prototiles imposes the condition that these labels are not all active at
  the same $x$.  On graphs with \eqref{eq:dependence}, this condition
  is equivalent to the family of tiling equations, regardless of $z$
  and the high outputs.
: proof
  Here $\operatorname{prev}(i)$ is the preceding channel in the chosen
  cyclic order, including the wrap from the first channel to the last.
  For every choice of maps
  $d_i:P_{\operatorname{prev}(i)}\to U_{i,j(i)}$, define
  $d_i[e]=d_i(e_{\operatorname{prev}(i)})$ on the cycle and $d_i[e]=0$
  off the cycle.  Include the prototile
  $$
   F_d=\{(0,\zeta,(w_i,e_i)_i): e\in P,\ \zeta\in\Cyc D,
                         \ w_i\bmod r_i=d_i[e]\}.
  $$
  Each $w_i$ ranges over all lifts of its specified low residue.
  For a target base $(x,k)$, the useful-output map is
  : equation \label{eq:cycle-map}
     e\longmapsto\bigl(e_i+g_{i,x}(k_i-d_i[e])\bigr)_i.
  The remaining output offsets have unique choices after $e$ is fixed.
  Thus $A\oplus F_d=G$ precisely when this map is a permutation for
  every $(x,k)$.

  If the chosen label at $i_0$ is inactive, its component in
  \eqref{eq:cycle-map} becomes $e_{i_0}+g_{i_0,x}(k_{i_0})$.
  It determines $e_{i_0}$ uniquely from the target.  Successive components
  around the cycle then determine all other $e_i$ uniquely; the initial
  component has no dependency left to check.  Off-cycle components are
  translations and can be solved independently.

  If all chosen labels are active at some $x$, choose witnesses
  $v_i,v_i'$ with $v_i-v_i'\in U_{i,j(i)}$ and
  $\epsilon_i=g_{i,x}(v_i)-g_{i,x}(v_i')\ne0$.
  Take $k_i=v_i$ on the cycle and choose maps with
  $$
   d_i(0)=0,\qquad
   d_i(\epsilon_{\operatorname{prev}(i)})=v_i-v_i'.
  $$
  The arguments are distinct, so such maps exist and are among those
  tested.  Extend $\epsilon$ by zero off the cycle.  The inputs $0$ and
  $\epsilon$ have the same image in \eqref{eq:cycle-map}, a contradiction
  to bijectivity.  All maps and all prototiles used are finite.

@fig:cycle illustrates the dependency cycle in this argument.

: figure[tbp]
  \centering
  \subcaptionbox{All selected labels active\label{fig:cycle-active}}[.47\linewidth]{%
    \begin{minipage}[c][2.8cm][c]{\linewidth}
      \centering
      \begin{tikzpicture}[font=\small,>=Latex,
        active/.style={circle,draw=figorange,fill=figorange!12,minimum size=1cm},
        inactive/.style={circle,draw=figgreen,fill=figgreen!12,minimum size=1cm},
        dependency/.style={->,semithick,draw=black!65}]
      \node[active] (a1) at (0,1) {$e_1$};
      \node[active] (a2) at (3,1) {$e_2$};
      \node[active] (a3) at (1.5,-.6) {$e_3$};
      \draw[dependency] (a1) -- node[above] {$d_2(e_1)$} (a2);
      \draw[dependency] (a2) -- node[right] {$d_3(e_2)$} (a3);
      \draw[dependency] (a3) -- node[left] {$d_1(e_3)$} (a1);
      \end{tikzpicture}
    \end{minipage}%
  }
  \hfil
  \subcaptionbox{Selected label at $1$ inactive\label{fig:cycle-inactive}}[.47\linewidth]{%
    \begin{minipage}[c][2.8cm][c]{\linewidth}
      \centering
      \begin{tikzpicture}[font=\small,>=Latex,
        active/.style={circle,draw=figorange,fill=figorange!12,minimum size=1cm},
        inactive/.style={circle,draw=figgreen,fill=figgreen!12,minimum size=1cm},
        dependency/.style={->,semithick,draw=black!65}]
      \node[inactive] (b1) at (0,1) {$e_1$};
      \node[active] (b2) at (3,1) {$e_2$};
      \node[active] (b3) at (1.5,-.6) {$e_3$};
      \draw[dependency] (b1) -- node[above] {$d_2(e_1)$} (b2);
      \draw[dependency] (b2) -- node[right] {$d_3(e_2)$} (b3);
      \draw[dashed,draw=black!35] (b3) -- (b1);
      \node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt,text=black!55] at (.7,.18) {$\times$};
      \end{tikzpicture}
    \end{minipage}%
  }
  \caption{A three-channel instance of the cycle test, adapted from
    \cite[Lemma~3.2]{OpenAI2026}. Arrows indicate dependence of an
    output component on another input in \eqref{eq:cycle-map}.
    (a) When all selected labels are active, some tested maps produce
    a collision. (b) An inactive selected label removes one dependency,
    allowing the components of the preimage to be recovered in order
    for every choice of the maps.}
  \label{fig:cycle}

Apply @lem:cycle to every word outside $\calW$, using the cycle
of all ordinary channels.  Also apply it to the pair $(\eta_t,n)$
with labels $*$ and $j$, for every $j\in\Sigma\setminus B_t$.
The resulting equations are exactly the conditions
: equation \label{eq:exclusions}
   \prod_{n\in I}\calA_n(x)\subseteq\calW,
   \qquad
   *\in\calA_{\eta_t}(x)\ \Longrightarrow\
   \calA_n(x)\subseteq B_t\quad(n\in I).
These conditions allow empty active sets.  The next section excludes
that degeneracy where it matters.

# Activation and Soundness @=sec:activation

***Activation tests*** are tiling equations designed to force nonempty active sets.
We adapt the activation tests of \cite[Section~4]{OpenAI2026} to the
residue set $S$ in \eqref{eq:residues}.  Their purpose is to make every
ordinary active set nonempty and each seed active somewhere.

## A Nonuniform List with Uniform Marginals

On $P_i$, let $\ind_e$ be the function equal to one at $e$ and zero
elsewhere, and let $1$ denote the constant function equal to one.
For each channel $i$, define
$$
 \omega_i=\ind_{(0,0)}-\ind_{(1,0)}-\ind_{(0,1)}+\ind_{(1,1)},
 \qquad \mu_i=1+\omega_i.
$$
We will use $\mu_i(e)$ as the ***multiplicity***, or number of occurrences,
of $e$ in a list.  These multiplicities are nonnegative and not all
equal.  Their ***marginals***, the sums over either coordinate, are uniform:
$$
 \sum_{\alpha\in\Cyc{a_i}}\mu_i(\alpha,\xi)=a_i,
 \qquad
 \sum_{\xi\in\Cyc{b_i}}\mu_i(\alpha,\xi)=b_i.
$$
Let $\Delta_i$ index a list $(e_\delta)_{\delta\in\Delta_i}$ of length
$a_ib_i$ having these multiplicities, with
$e_\delta=(e_\delta^a,e_\delta^b)$.
For each fixed $\xi$, choose a bijection from the indices with
$e_\delta^b=\xi$ to $\Cyc{a_i}$, and express it as
$\delta\mapsto e_\delta^a+\rho_i^a(\delta)$.
Independently, for each fixed $\alpha$, choose a bijection from the
indices with $e_\delta^a=\alpha$ to $\Cyc{b_i}$, expressed as
$\delta\mapsto e_\delta^b+\rho_i^b(\delta)$.
Thus both maps
: equation \label{eq:orderings}
  \begin{aligned}
   \{\delta\colon e_\delta^b=\xi\}&\longrightarrow\Cyc{a_i},
     &\delta&\longmapsto e_\delta^a+\rho_i^a(\delta),\\
   \{\delta\colon e_\delta^a=\alpha\}&\longrightarrow\Cyc{b_i},
     &\delta&\longmapsto e_\delta^b+\rho_i^b(\delta)
  \end{aligned}
are bijections.  Repeated entries in the list have different indices.

@fig:activation gives a small example of this list on
$(\Cyc2)\times(\Cyc3)$. Only the multiplicity pattern is illustrated;
the prime choices in the full encoding remain those specified in
@sec:encoding.

: figure[tbp]
