    are small at this scale.}
  \label{fig:connected}

## Why the Dimension Is Optimal

: proposition @=prop:planar
  Given a finite nonempty $F\subset\Z^2$, it is decidable whether
  $F$ tiles $\Z^2$ by translations.

: proof
  Bhattacharya proved that every such prototile admitting a tiling also
  admits a tiling invariant under a finite-index subgroup of $\Z^2$
  [[Bhattacharya]].  Every finite-index subgroup contains $m\Z^2$
  for some positive integer $m$.
  Run two searches in parallel.  The first enumerates $m$ and all
  Boolean assignments on $(\Cyc m)^2$, checking the periodic versions
  of \eqref{eq:boolean-tiling}.  It halts if a periodic tiling exists.
  The second searches for an unsatisfiable finite subsystem, as in
  the membership argument.  It halts if no tiling exists.
  The periodicity theorem ensures that exactly one search eventually
  halts, so this is a decision algorithm.

The one-dimensional case reduces to this planar problem by replacing
$F\subset\Z$ by $F\times\{0\}\subset\Z^2$.
For every fixed $d>3$, the three-dimensional hardness result extends
by replacing $F$ with $F\times\{0\}^{d-3}$ and taking slices.
Thus three is precisely the least lattice dimension with undecidable
translational monotiling, even with a connected input prototile.

## Arbitrary Real Translations of Unit-Cube Unions

We now prove the Euclidean formulation using the tiling convention
from @sec:definitions.  For $v\in\R^d$, write
$\|v\|_\infty=\max_j|v_j|$ for the ***sup norm***.
Discrete-to-Euclidean tiling reductions also appear in
\cite[Section~2]{GTPeriodic}; the unit-cube existence equivalence
needed here has the direct sampling proof below.

: lemma @=lem:rounding
  For every finite nonempty $F\subset\Z^d$, the set
  $\Omega=F+[0,1]^d$ tiles $\R^d$ by real translations if and only
  if $F$ tiles $\Z^d$ by integer translations.

: proof
  An integer tiling thickens to a Euclidean tiling.
  Conversely, suppose translates $a+\Omega$, $a\in A\subset\R^d$,
  form an almost-everywhere tiling.  Distinct points of $A$ have
  sup-norm distance at least one, since otherwise translates of a
  fixed unit cube of $\Omega$ overlap in positive volume.  Hence
  $A$ is countable.

  Choose $\theta\in(0,1)^d$ so that every point of $\theta+\Z^d$
  has coverage multiplicity one and $\theta_j\not\equiv a_j\pmod1$
  for every $a\in A$ and every coordinate $j$.
  Such a choice exists: the excluded coverage sets, translated by
  integer vectors, form a countable union of null sets; the excluded
  coordinate hyperplanes are also countably many null sets.
  Define $b(a)\in\Z^d$ by $b(a)_j=\lceil a_j-\theta_j\rceil$.
  Intersecting a translate with the sampled grid gives exactly
  $$
   (a+\Omega)\cap(\theta+\Z^d)=\theta+b(a)+F.
  $$
  Every sampled point lies in exactly one such set.  The map
  $a\mapsto b(a)$ is injective, because equal rounded vectors would
  make two nonempty sampled copies coincide.  Consequently
  $\{b(a):a\in A\}\oplus F=\Z^d$.

: corollary @=cor:euclidean
  Given a finite nonempty $F\subset\Z^3$, the problem of deciding
  whether its unit-cube union $F+[0,1]^3$ tiles $\R^3$ by arbitrary
  real translations is co-RE-complete.
  This remains true when $F$ is required to be connected, in which
  case its unit-cube union is connected and has connected interior.
  For the analogous class of unit-square unions in $\R^2$, the
  problem is decidable.

: proof
  Apply @lem:rounding to @thm:main,prop:planar.
  The lattice paths in $F$ give chains of cubes sharing faces, so
  both the union and its interior are connected.

The planar Euclidean decidability conclusion is also known directly
from \cite[Corollary~1.1]{RationalPolygons}.

: remark
  The sampling argument produces a new tiling with integer translations.
  It does not assert that the original set of real translations was integral,
  and no preservation of its period group is used.

